course: dictionary
lesson: 15031
title: Rules that limit
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A restriction is a rule that limits what you can do. The word comes from restrict, which means to keep something within limits. «There is an age restriction on that film, so younger children cannot watch it.» The clue is age: the rule limits who can watch.
explain: Restriction can also mean the act of limiting something. «The restriction of water use helped the town through the dry summer.» Here the word means cutting down how much water people used, not just the rule itself.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Height restriction» tells you there is a limit on how tall you can be. «Under restrictions» tells you people must follow rules that limit them. «Lift the restriction» means remove the rule, so things are allowed again.
explain: Try swapping in your meaning. «The speed restriction on this road is 30 miles per hour.» Replace restriction with a rule that limits speed: «The rule that limits speed on this road is 30 miles per hour.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A restriction is a rule that limits what you can do.
fact: «Restriction» can also mean the act of limiting something, as in «the restriction of noise».
fact: «Age restriction» means you must be a certain age to do or see something.
fact: «Height restriction» means you must be a certain height to go on a ride, like a roller coaster.
fact: In maths, a restriction may limit which numbers are allowed in an answer, such as «whole numbers only».
exercise: «The cinema has an age restriction on that film.» What does restriction mean here? | A poster of the film on the wall | A free drink with your ticket | *A rule that limits who can watch it | A prize given for the best film
exercise: «Because of the dry weather, there are water restrictions in our town.» What does restriction mean? | A flood of heavy rain | *A rule that limits how much water people can use | A new water park opening soon | A warning about cold, wet weather
exercise: What does «lift the restriction» mean? | To lift a heavy box off the floor | *To remove the rule so things are allowed again | To build a higher fence | To make a rule stricter
test: «You must be at least 1.2 metres tall for the ride, so there is a height restriction.» What does height restriction mean? | A tall building in the town | *A rule that limits how tall you must be to ride | A ride that goes very high | A measuring tape for your height
test: Which sentence uses restriction to mean the act of limiting something? | Cars were moving very fast | *The restriction of traffic made the park quieter | The park was full of birds | The traffic lights were red
exam: In maths, a restriction says the answer must be a whole number. What does this restriction do? | It makes the answer bigger | It adds two numbers together | It hides the answer from the reader | *It limits which numbers are allowed
